---
/* The viewport — section three.

   Two references folded together: the chrome and posture of a 3D editor (a tool
   rail, a wireframe form on a shadow, property panels, a live readout) carrying
   the process stages as its content. Scrolling steps the tool: the rail advances,
   the form in the middle changes and keeps turning, the right panel swaps to
   that stage's deliverables, and the readout counts the rotation.

   The form is drawn from REAL VERTICES, projected each frame — see lab.js. A
   CSS rotate on a flat SVG would betray itself the moment the shape turned past
   a few degrees, because the far edges would not foreshorten. Five forms, one
   per stage, so the object itself reports which step you are on before you read
   a word of it.

   Monochrome on the site's own ground rather than the reference's lime. One
   thing highlighted at a time is what makes an instrument read as precise; a
   second accent colour would be the loudest thing on a page that has spent four
   sections staying quiet. */

/* THE RAIL ICONS, KEYED TO THE FORM AND NOT TO A POSITION.

   These were five CSS rules selected by :nth-child — a square, the same square
   rotated 45 degrees, a circle, a squircle and a dot. Two problems with that,
   and the second is the one that bit. A rotated square is a DIAMOND, so the
   pyramid stage was labelled with a shape it does not contain. And because the
   selectors keyed off position rather than content, the icon could never be
   wrong in a way the markup could catch: reorder the stages and every glyph
   silently describes its neighbour.

   Drawn per form instead, and looked up by `s.form`, so an icon is now a
   property of the shape rather than of where the shape happens to sit. A
   triangle is also not expressible as a border-radius, which is the honest
   reason the diamond was there in the first place. */
const ICON = {
  cube:     '<rect x="3.2" y="3.2" width="9.6" height="9.6" rx="1" />',
  /* apex up, sitting on its base — the same posture the wireframe holds */
  pyramid:  '<path d="M8 2.6 L13.6 13.2 H2.4 Z" stroke-linejoin="round" />',
  sphere:   '<circle cx="8" cy="8" r="5" />',
  /* an ellipse for the near rim and the two walls dropping from it: the
     silhouette of a cylinder, which a rounded rectangle is not */
  cylinder: '<ellipse cx="8" cy="4.4" rx="4.4" ry="1.8" />' +
            '<path d="M3.6 4.4v7.2a4.4 1.8 0 0 0 8.8 0V4.4" />',
  /* four of the lattice's own points, so the last icon is a sample of the last
     form rather than a full stop */
  points:   '<g fill="currentColor" stroke="none">' +
            '<circle cx="5.2" cy="5.2" r="1.15" /><circle cx="10.8" cy="5.2" r="1.15" />' +
            '<circle cx="5.2" cy="10.8" r="1.15" /><circle cx="10.8" cy="10.8" r="1.15" /></g>',
};

const STAGES = [
  { n: '01', h: 'Discovery', form: 'cube',
    items: ['Creative brief', 'Brand research', 'Mood boards'] },
  { n: '02', h: 'Concept',   form: 'pyramid',
    items: ['Visual direction', 'Art direction', 'Storyboarding'] },
  { n: '03', h: 'Design',    form: 'sphere',
    items: ['UI/UX craft', 'Motion design', 'Interaction design'] },
  { n: '04', h: 'Develop',   form: 'cylinder',
    items: ['Creative coding', 'WebGL & 3D', 'Animations'] },
  { n: '05', h: 'Polish',    form: 'points',
    items: ['Micro-interactions', 'Performance', 'Pixel perfect'] },
];
---
<!-- The stage data rides on an ATTRIBUTE, not a JSON script block.
     Astro owns script tags — it hoists and bundles them — so a data block
     placed here never reaches the DOM, and lab.js threw on the missing node
     before it rendered anything at all. Attributes pass through untouched. -->
<section class="lab" data-lab data-lab-data={JSON.stringify(STAGES)}>
  <div class="lab-pin">
    <!-- The corner chrome is gone. It read "Viewport — perspective" on the left
         and counted "01 / 05" on the right, and both were instrument-cosplay:
         two more things to read in the top band of a screen that now opens with
         a real header. The count survives where it is actually useful, inside
         the timeline in the right panel, next to the thing it measures.

         TWO LINES, ONE SIZE. It was a 10.5px uppercase kicker over a 38px
         headline, which is two different kinds of type doing one job — and
         section two states itself as two equal lines ("Four disciplines." /
         "One practice."), so a small-then-big pairing here made the two
         sections sound like they were written by different people. Same
         cadence now: one size, one weight, a claim and its correction.

         The sentence is a pivot on one preposition. "design WITH" is the
         tooling, which has changed completely and is worth saying out loud in
         2026; "design BY" is the method underneath it, which has not. Naming AI
         and then refusing to make it the point is the whole position — the five
         stages below are what the second line is pointing at. -->
    <header class="lab-head">
      <h2 class="lab-h">
        <span>AI changed the tools I design with.</span>
        <span>Not the principles I design by.</span>
      </h2>
    </header>

    <aside class="lab-rail">
      <p class="lab-lbl">Forms</p>
      <ul class="lab-forms">
        {STAGES.map((s, i) => (
          <li class="lab-form" data-lab-form={i} aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" set:html={ICON[s.form]} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <p class="lab-lbl">Stages</p>
      <ul class="lab-tools">
        {STAGES.map((s, i) => (
          <li class="lab-tool" data-lab-tool={i}>
            <span class="lab-tool-n">{s.n}</span>
            <span class="lab-tool-h">{s.h}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <div class="lab-stage">
      <span class="lab-grid" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <svg class="lab-wire" viewBox="-120 -120 240 240" aria-hidden="true">
        <g data-lab-wire></g>
      </svg>
      <span class="lab-shadow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <div class="lab-read">
        <span class="lab-lbl">Rotation</span>
        <p class="lab-deg" data-lab-deg>0°</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="lab-panel">
      <p class="lab-lbl">Deliverables</p>
      <ul class="lab-tiles">
        {[0, 1, 2].map(i => (
          <li class="lab-tile" data-lab-tile={i}>
            <span class="lab-tile-i" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="lab-tile-t"></span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <!-- THE TIMELINE.

           A 2px line with a fill on it was a loading bar wearing a label — it
           measured the section without belonging to it. This is the strip every
           3D and animation tool puts along its bottom edge: a track, a keyframe
           per stage, and a playhead riding the scrub. It says the same number
           and it says it in the section's own language, which is the difference
           between a progress bar and an instrument.

           The keys fill as the playhead passes them, so the strip carries a
           history as well as a position — where you are, and what you have
           already been through. -->
      <p class="lab-lbl">Timeline</p>
      <div class="lab-time" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="lab-time-rail"></span>
        <span class="lab-time-fill" data-lab-bar></span>
        {STAGES.map((s, i) => (
          <span class="lab-key" data-lab-key={i}
                style={`--at:${((i + 0.5) / STAGES.length) * 100}%`}></span>
        ))}
        <span class="lab-play" data-lab-play></span>
      </div>
      <p class="lab-time-read">
        <span data-lab-count>01</span><span class="lab-time-of"> / 05</span>
        <span class="lab-time-pct" data-lab-pct>0%</span>
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>

</section>
